# Generated files — kept in git because a tool needs them, not because anyone
# reads them. Marking them collapses the diff on GitHub and keeps them out of
# the repository's language statistics. They are still versioned, still diffable
# with `git diff`, and still required.
#
# drizzle-kit writes a full snapshot of the schema after every migration and
# diffs the TypeScript schema against the latest one to produce the next `.sql`.
# Deleting them does not shrink anything worth shrinking: the runtime migrator
# never opens them (it reads `_journal.json` and the `.sql` files), but without
# a baseline `drizzle-kit generate` decides the schema does not exist and emits
# a migration that recreates every table. Migration 0011 is two `CREATE INDEX`
# lines; its snapshot is 3475.
server/src/db/migrations/meta/*.json linguist-generated=true

# Images are binary. `git diff --stat` already reports them as `Bin N -> M bytes`,
# but tools that count lines themselves — review UIs, `wc -l`, "lines changed"
# badges — read the newline bytes inside the compressed stream and report a
# screenshot as thousands of changed lines. On 2026-08-26 the five design PNGs
# under `specs/assets/` were counted as 19 206 lines against ~10 400 lines of
# actual code, which is what a reader saw first. Marking them `binary` is the
# explicit form of what git already infers, and it is what those tools read.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary

# npm writes this; nobody reads it. `agent-runner` uses npm (not pnpm) like
# `reviewer-core`, `e2e` and `mcp`, so its lockfile is a committed 2 017-line
# generated file that would otherwise dominate the diff of any change near it.
agent-runner/package-lock.json linguist-generated=true
